%% The Topic 5 key diagrams, redrawn simply — RN volume only (included by RN.gsm).

## The key diagrams

Four diagrams carry most of Topic 5. Learn to draw each one from memory in **under two minutes**. A diagram placed early in an answer shows the examiner the whole argument at a glance and earns credit in its own right.

::: fig bars | Pakistan in the Global Gender Gap Report (rank; lower is better).
@colour 2F7D4F
2006 (of 115): 112
2017 (of 144): 143 *
2021 (of 156): 153
2023 (of 146): 142
2025 (of 148): 148 *
2026 (of 145): 143
:::

**Draw it in:** "second-worst" (2018) and the 2025 report (2026). Add the 2026 sub-index ranks: economy 144th, education 130th, politics 107th.

::: fig flow | Fathalla's road to maternal death (1987), shortened.
@per 4
Born female and poor
Anaemic, unschooled
Married young
No family planning
No antenatal care
Complication at birth
Three delays: decide, reach, receive
Death — preventable
:::

**Draw it in:** the health questions (2017, 2023) and the road to maternal death (2024). Pakistan's ratio is **186** per 100,000 live births (PMMS 2019).

::: fig cycle | Why laws fail women — the enforcement gap.
@centre The enforcement gap
Rights unknown
Police delay complaints
Slow courts, few convictions
Family pressure, jirgas
Under-funded commissions
Norms override law
:::

**Draw it in:** law and human rights (2020). Every list of laws must end with this gap.

::: fig venn | Overlapping disadvantage in Pakistan.
@centre Poor, rural, minority woman with a disability
Gender: patriarchy, mobility limits
Place and class: rural, landless
Identity and body: minority; disability
:::

**Draw it in:** intersectionality (2026), and any status question where "women" are not one group.
